[Event "Chapter 1: The Traxler Main Line (6.Kxf2) / Study 1: 6.Kxf2 Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/6b382454-ec65-40ee-bfea-338bf1f2e8f2/studies/34d66538-2132-44f3-baa0-67647e1ce390/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Traxler! This is an aggressive gambit against the Fried Liver Attack. There are more than 25 million games played in the Fried Liver, so the chances you'll get to play your Traxler are really high! White starts with 1.e4. We'll play 1...e5 to lay claim to the center as well.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks our e5-pawn. Let's defend our center.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White goes for the Italian Game. We'll develop our other knight, because we're not afraid of the Fried Liver.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {The Fried Liver Attack. White converges on f7. What can we do? We can go for a counterattack! This is a gambit of our f7-pawn, but if White takes, they can get into a world of trouble.} 4...Bc5 5.Nxf7 {About 63% of games go like this. White cannot resist forking us. But this is actually a mistake. We take their f-pawn with check! This is The Traxler! The point is not material, but the fact that we'll be immediately attacking the king.} 5...Bxf2+ 6.Kxf2 {Close to 60% of games feature 6.Kxf2, which is why we're seeing this first in our course. Since the king is vulnerable, we'll continue giving checks with aggression. Our knight jumps in, capturing the e-pawn! The white king won't know what to do. A good rule to remember is that the king loses by going to any light square!} 6...Nxe4+ 7.Ke3 {The other move that does not lose, but that also seems to be not so unpopular, is 7.Ke3. White plays this about 28% of the time, which is north of 270,000 games. White is in serious trouble after we play Qh4! Stockfish can defend this, but 99% of the people online aren't Stockfish!} 7...Qh4 8.Qf3 {This move doesn't work. Retreating our attacked knight wins the game. Our plan is ...Nd4 to continue the checkmate attack.} 8...Nf6 9.Nxh8 {So, let's say the knight takes our rook.} 9...Nd4 {We can attack the queen. The attack on the queen is one thing, but the point is there are so many pieces around White's king. 10.Qg3 Nf5+ is a nasty fork.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Traxler Main Line (6.Kxf2) / Study 1: 6.Kxf2 Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/6b382454-ec65-40ee-bfea-338bf1f2e8f2/studies/34d66538-2132-44f3-baa0-67647e1ce390/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Traxler! This is an aggressive gambit against the Fried Liver Attack. There are more than 25 million games played in the Fried Liver, so the chances you'll get to play your Traxler are really high! White starts with 1.e4. We'll play 1...e5 to lay claim to the center as well.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks our e5-pawn. Let's defend our center.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White goes for the Italian Game. We'll develop our other knight, because we're not afraid of the Fried Liver.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {The Fried Liver Attack. White converges on f7. What can we do? We can go for a counterattack! This is a gambit of our f7-pawn, but if White takes, they can get into a world of trouble.} 4...Bc5 5.Nxf7 {About 63% of games go like this. White cannot resist forking us. But this is actually a mistake. We take their f-pawn with check! This is The Traxler! The point is not material, but the fact that we'll be immediately attacking the king.} 5...Bxf2+ 6.Kxf2 {Close to 60% of games feature 6.Kxf2, which is why we're seeing this first in our course. Since the king is vulnerable, we'll continue giving checks with aggression. Our knight jumps in, capturing the e-pawn! The white king won't know what to do. A good rule to remember is that the king loses by going to any light square!} 6...Nxe4+ 7.Ke3 {The other move that does not lose, but that also seems to be not so unpopular, is 7.Ke3. White plays this about 28% of the time, which is north of 270,000 games. White is in serious trouble after we play Qh4! Stockfish can defend this, but 99% of the people online aren't Stockfish!} 7...Qh4 8.Nxh8 {The knight takes on h8. This leads to a beautiful finish. We have a forced checkmate, starting with Qf4+.} 8...Qf4+ 9.Kd3 {The king goes to d3. We'll play Nb4+ and force the king backwards. Mate is near.} 9...Nb4+ 10.Ke2 {The only move.} 10...Qf2# {Checkmate. Many finishes like this are possible if White messes up!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Traxler Main Line (6.Kxf2) / Study 1: 6.Kxf2 Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/6b382454-ec65-40ee-bfea-338bf1f2e8f2/studies/34d66538-2132-44f3-baa0-67647e1ce390/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Traxler! This is an aggressive gambit against the Fried Liver Attack. There are more than 25 million games played in the Fried Liver, so the chances you'll get to play your Traxler are really high! White starts with 1.e4. We'll play 1...e5 to lay claim to the center as well.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks our e5-pawn. Let's defend our center.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White goes for the Italian Game. We'll develop our other knight, because we're not afraid of the Fried Liver.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {The Fried Liver Attack. White converges on f7. What can we do? We can go for a counterattack! This is a gambit of our f7-pawn, but if White takes, they can get into a world of trouble.} 4...Bc5 5.Nxf7 {About 63% of games go like this. White cannot resist forking us. But this is actually a mistake. We take their f-pawn with check! This is The Traxler! The point is not material, but the fact that we'll be immediately attacking the king.} 5...Bxf2+ 6.Kxf2 {Close to 60% of games feature 6.Kxf2, which is why we're seeing this first in our course. Since the king is vulnerable, we'll continue giving checks with aggression. Our knight jumps in, capturing the e-pawn! The white king won't know what to do. A good rule to remember is that the king loses by going to any light square!} 6...Nxe4+ 7.Kg1 {The most popular king move is the safest. 7.Kg1 happens in about 30% of games. The king is safe from checks for now. Our queen will arrive on h4 and threaten mate on f2!} 7...Qh4 8.g3 {A simple way of defending is 8.g3, which has been seen in close to 50,000 games. White can defend the f2-square with the queen, of course. But 8.Qf3 or Qf1 is met with 8...Rf8 and suddenly the knight on f7 is pinned! ...d5 and ...Nd4 would also cause problems. We'll take on g3! Retreating is not an option! We have a draw at the very least, and a checkmate at best!} 8...Nxg3 9.Nxh8 {A lower percentage of games go like this. But we don't care about material in the Traxler: we care about attacking the white king! Our queen's knight leaps into the center.} 9...Nd4 10.hxg3 {White is advised to capture on g3 now. We'll recapture with check.} 10...Qxg3+ 11.Kf1 {The king moves away.} 11...Qf4+ {The best thing we have here is a draw by repetition, if White can find all the correct moves, of course!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Traxler Main Line (6.Kxf2) / Study 1: 6.Kxf2 Lines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/6b382454-ec65-40ee-bfea-338bf1f2e8f2/studies/34d66538-2132-44f3-baa0-67647e1ce390/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Traxler! This is an aggressive gambit against the Fried Liver Attack. There are more than 25 million games played in the Fried Liver, so the chances you'll get to play your Traxler are really high! White starts with 1.e4. We'll play 1...e5 to lay claim to the center as well.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks our e5-pawn. Let's defend our center.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White goes for the Italian Game. We'll develop our other knight, because we're not afraid of the Fried Liver.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {The Fried Liver Attack. White converges on f7. What can we do? We can go for a counterattack! This is a gambit of our f7-pawn, but if White takes, they can get into a world of trouble.} 4...Bc5 5.Nxf7 {About 63% of games go like this. White cannot resist forking us. But this is actually a mistake. We take their f-pawn with check! This is The Traxler! The point is not material, but the fact that we'll be immediately attacking the king.} 5...Bxf2+ 6.Kxf2 {Close to 60% of games feature 6.Kxf2, which is why we're seeing this first in our course. Since the king is vulnerable, we'll continue giving checks with aggression. Our knight jumps in, capturing the e-pawn! The white king won't know what to do. A good rule to remember is that the king loses by going to any light square!} 6...Nxe4+ 7.Ke3 {The other move that does not lose, but that also seems to be not so unpopular, is 7.Ke3. White plays this about 28% of the time, which is north of 270,000 games. White is in serious trouble after we play Qh4! Stockfish can defend this, but 99% of the people online aren't Stockfish!} 7...Qh4 8.g3 {This move is the best, but White has to find it. We can take on g3. Very important: with the idea of ...Qd4+ because ...Qxc4 isn't our goal. We want to attack the king. Material is out of the question for now.} 8...Nxg3 9.hxg3 {White recaptures. And we give a check to force the king to a bad file.} 9...Qd4+ 10.Kf3 {On the f-file, we can do things with tempo.} 10...O-O {White's pieces are hanging. Believe it or not, the only move that doesn't lose here is 11.Rh4! We would then continue with e4+ and a wild position ensues which the engine evaluates as approximately equal. Your opponents are not engines though!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Traxler Main Line (6.Kxf2) / Study 1: 6.Kxf2 Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/6b382454-ec65-40ee-bfea-338bf1f2e8f2/studies/34d66538-2132-44f3-baa0-67647e1ce390/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Traxler! This is an aggressive gambit against the Fried Liver Attack. There are more than 25 million games played in the Fried Liver, so the chances you'll get to play your Traxler are really high! White starts with 1.e4. We'll play 1...e5 to lay claim to the center as well.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks our e5-pawn. Let's defend our center.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White goes for the Italian Game. We'll develop our other knight, because we're not afraid of the Fried Liver.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {The Fried Liver Attack. White converges on f7. What can we do? We can go for a counterattack! This is a gambit of our f7-pawn, but if White takes, they can get into a world of trouble.} 4...Bc5 5.Nxf7 {About 63% of games go like this. White cannot resist forking us. But this is actually a mistake. We take their f-pawn with check! This is The Traxler! The point is not material, but the fact that we'll be immediately attacking the king.} 5...Bxf2+ 6.Kxf2 {Close to 60% of games feature 6.Kxf2, which is why we're seeing this first in our course. Since the king is vulnerable, we'll continue giving checks with aggression. Our knight jumps in, capturing the e-pawn! The white king won't know what to do. A good rule to remember is that the king loses by going to any light square!} 6...Nxe4+ 7.Kg1 {The most popular king move is the safest. 7.Kg1 happens in about 30% of games. The king is safe from checks for now. Our queen will arrive on h4 and threaten mate on f2!} 7...Qh4 8.g3 {A simple way of defending is 8.g3, which has been seen in close to 50,000 games. White can defend the f2-square with the queen, of course. But 8.Qf3 or Qf1 is met with 8...Rf8 and suddenly the knight on f7 is pinned! ...d5 and ...Nd4 would also cause problems. We'll take on g3! Retreating is not an option! We have a draw at the very least, and a checkmate at best!} 8...Nxg3 9.hxg3 {The pawn recaptures. This move is the most popular. We'll take on g3 and give check.} 9...Qxg3+ 10.Kf1 {The king is forced to f1.} 10...Rf8 {And we can pin the knight on f7. We have ideas of d5 to distract the bishop on c4 but also to play Bh3+. White has to pray for a draw.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Traxler Main Line (6.Kxf2) / Study 1: 6.Kxf2 Lines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/6b382454-ec65-40ee-bfea-338bf1f2e8f2/studies/34d66538-2132-44f3-baa0-67647e1ce390/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the Traxler! This is an aggressive gambit against the Fried Liver Attack. There are more than 25 million games played in the Fried Liver, so the chances you'll get to play your Traxler are really high! White starts with 1.e4. We'll play 1...e5 to lay claim to the center as well.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks our e5-pawn. Let's defend our center.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White goes for the Italian Game. We'll develop our other knight, because we're not afraid of the Fried Liver.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {The Fried Liver Attack. White converges on f7. What can we do? We can go for a counterattack! This is a gambit of our f7-pawn, but if White takes, they can get into a world of trouble.} 4...Bc5 5.Nxf7 {About 63% of games go like this. White cannot resist forking us. But this is actually a mistake. We take their f-pawn with check! This is The Traxler! The point is not material, but the fact that we'll be immediately attacking the king.} 5...Bxf2+ 6.Kxf2 {Close to 60% of games feature 6.Kxf2, which is why we're seeing this first in our course. Since the king is vulnerable, we'll continue giving checks with aggression. Our knight jumps in, capturing the e-pawn! The white king won't know what to do. A good rule to remember is that the king loses by going to any light square!} 6...Nxe4+ 7.Ke1 {This is a terrible move: White is putting the pieces back on the home squares to play another game. This happens in roughly 26% of games. Qh4+ is a lethal check. Since the king is on a bad square, g3 doesn't work.} 7...Qh4+ 8.g3 {White blocks.} 8...Nxg3 {We have a winning attack. We're threatening a discovered check on the king. White can't even take our knight as the rook would fall with check and then more material will go afterwards.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Doesn't Take The Bishop (6.Kf1) / Study 1: 6.Kf1 Lines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/7babac54-4126-47de-9a3f-ddd6aad8d117/studies/4eca023d-3b94-463d-b05b-6da99faf0c72/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study you'll learn how to crush people with the Traxler in the positions that may arise after we take on f2 and White goes 6.Kf1. White starts off in the most traditional way, pushing the king's pawn in the center. We'll mirror White's opening with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight, targeting the e5 pawn. We'll develop our knight as well, defending e5.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The Italian game, one of the most popular openings at any level. We develop our other knight to f6, attacking the e4-pawn.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {White decides to enter the Fried Liver! ...Bc5 is The Traxler counterattack!} 4...Bc5 5.Nxf7 {This is our second study looking at 5.Nxf7. It looks like we have too many pieces hanging, but we'll sacrifice another to put the White king in danger: Bxf2+!} 5...Bxf2+ 6.Kf1 {In this chapter we will analyze the positions arising from Kf1, which is the best move, but White's position remains in peril. It's not the most common move, however, played 41% of the time, which is 662 thousand games. White is using our bishop as a shield. Let's go ahead and remove our queen from danger.} 6...Qe7 7.Nxh8 {White decides to go up a rook by capturing on h8. Now that we we have sacrificed the rook, let's focus on attacking the king! 7...d5 opens the position even more. Our light-squared bishop is unleashed with a tempo!} 7...d5 8.Bxd5 {White has missed the point of 7...d5. This move blunders the queen.} 8...Bg4 {And we trap the queen!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Doesn't Take The Bishop (6.Kf1) / Study 1: 6.Kf1 Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/7babac54-4126-47de-9a3f-ddd6aad8d117/studies/4eca023d-3b94-463d-b05b-6da99faf0c72/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study you'll learn how to crush people with the Traxler in the positions that may arise after we take on f2 and White goes 6.Kf1. White starts off in the most traditional way, pushing the king's pawn in the center. We'll mirror White's opening with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight, targeting the e5 pawn. We'll develop our knight as well, defending e5.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The Italian game, one of the most popular openings at any level. We develop our other knight to f6, attacking the e4-pawn.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {White decides to enter the Fried Liver! ...Bc5 is The Traxler counterattack!} 4...Bc5 5.Nxf7 {This is our second study looking at 5.Nxf7. It looks like we have too many pieces hanging, but we'll sacrifice another to put the White king in danger: Bxf2+!} 5...Bxf2+ 6.Kf1 {In this chapter we will analyze the positions arising from Kf1, which is the best move, but White's position remains in peril. It's not the most common move, however, played 41% of the time, which is 662 thousand games. White is using our bishop as a shield. Let's go ahead and remove our queen from danger.} 6...Qe7 7.Nxh8 {White decides to go up a rook by capturing on h8. Now that we we have sacrificed the rook, let's focus on attacking the king! 7...d5 opens the position even more. Our light-squared bishop is unleashed with a tempo!} 7...d5 8.exd5 {White recaptures and gains a tempo on our knight. We'll jump into the center with our knight, with the threat of Bg4, trapping White's queen.} 8...Nd4 9.c3 {9.c3 is a critical move, because it opens an escape diagonal for the queen in case of Bg4 and creates a threat on the knight. Bg4 is still a very strong developing move.} 9...Bg4 10.Qa4+ {White saves their queen while giving a check. Let's calmly block the check, while potentially freeing up the f6 square for our queen. The idea of opening up the f-file is very important.} 10...Nd7 11.cxd4 {A common blunder... We are currently down 9 points of material, but our attack is crushing.} 11...Qf6 {Qf6 sets up a deadly discovered check. White now can only delay checkmate with ridiculous moves giving away material.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Doesn't Take The Bishop (6.Kf1) / Study 1: 6.Kf1 Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/7babac54-4126-47de-9a3f-ddd6aad8d117/studies/4eca023d-3b94-463d-b05b-6da99faf0c72/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study you'll learn how to crush people with the Traxler in the positions that may arise after we take on f2 and White goes 6.Kf1. White starts off in the most traditional way, pushing the king's pawn in the center. We'll mirror White's opening with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight, targeting the e5 pawn. We'll develop our knight as well, defending e5.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The Italian game, one of the most popular openings at any level. We develop our other knight to f6, attacking the e4-pawn.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {White decides to enter the Fried Liver! ...Bc5 is The Traxler counterattack!} 4...Bc5 5.Nxf7 {This is our second study looking at 5.Nxf7. It looks like we have too many pieces hanging, but we'll sacrifice another to put the White king in danger: Bxf2+!} 5...Bxf2+ 6.Kf1 {In this chapter we will analyze the positions arising from Kf1, which is the best move, but White's position remains in peril. It's not the most common move, however, played 41% of the time, which is 662 thousand games. White is using our bishop as a shield. Let's go ahead and remove our queen from danger.} 6...Qe7 7.Nxh8 {White decides to go up a rook by capturing on h8. Now that we we have sacrificed the rook, let's focus on attacking the king! 7...d5 opens the position even more. Our light-squared bishop is unleashed with a tempo!} 7...d5 8.exd5 {White recaptures and gains a tempo on our knight. We'll jump into the center with our knight, with the threat of Bg4, trapping White's queen.} 8...Nd4 9.c3 {9.c3 is a critical move, because it opens an escape diagonal for the queen in case of Bg4 and creates a threat on the knight. Bg4 is still a very strong developing move.} 9...Bg4 10.Qa4+ {White saves their queen while giving a check. Let's calmly block the check, while potentially freeing up the f6 square for our queen. The idea of opening up the f-file is very important.} 10...Nd7 11.Kxf2 {11.Kxf2 is by far the funniest line in the chapter... just that only we get a laugh! The White king is left exposed on f2. Now 11...Qh4+ is a very tricky check.} 11...Qh4+ 12.g3 {White tries to block the check with the pawn, hoping to create a shield. Now the light squares around the king are really weak. We switch back with the queen. Qf6+ basically guarantees the win for Black. Now White is forced to the back rank.} 12...Qf6+ 13.Ke1 {Notice how our bishop prevents the White king from escaping to the queenside.} 13...Qf5 {Qf5! A slow move that secures the advantage: we're infiltrating on the light squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Doesn't Take The Bishop (6.Kf1) / Study 1: 6.Kf1 Lines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/7babac54-4126-47de-9a3f-ddd6aad8d117/studies/4eca023d-3b94-463d-b05b-6da99faf0c72/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study you'll learn how to crush people with the Traxler in the positions that may arise after we take on f2 and White goes 6.Kf1. White starts off in the most traditional way, pushing the king's pawn in the center. We'll mirror White's opening with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight, targeting the e5 pawn. We'll develop our knight as well, defending e5.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The Italian game, one of the most popular openings at any level. We develop our other knight to f6, attacking the e4-pawn.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {White decides to enter the Fried Liver! ...Bc5 is The Traxler counterattack!} 4...Bc5 5.Nxf7 {This is our second study looking at 5.Nxf7. It looks like we have too many pieces hanging, but we'll sacrifice another to put the White king in danger: Bxf2+!} 5...Bxf2+ 6.Kf1 {In this chapter we will analyze the positions arising from Kf1, which is the best move, but White's position remains in peril. It's not the most common move, however, played 41% of the time, which is 662 thousand games. White is using our bishop as a shield. Let's go ahead and remove our queen from danger.} 6...Qe7 7.Nxh8 {White decides to go up a rook by capturing on h8. Now that we we have sacrificed the rook, let's focus on attacking the king! 7...d5 opens the position even more. Our light-squared bishop is unleashed with a tempo!} 7...d5 8.exd5 {White recaptures and gains a tempo on our knight. We'll jump into the center with our knight, with the threat of Bg4, trapping White's queen.} 8...Nd4 9.c3 {9.c3 is a critical move, because it opens an escape diagonal for the queen in case of Bg4 and creates a threat on the knight. Bg4 is still a very strong developing move.} 9...Bg4 10.Qa4+ {White saves their queen while giving a check. Let's calmly block the check, while potentially freeing up the f6 square for our queen. The idea of opening up the f-file is very important.} 10...Nd7 11.Kxf2 {11.Kxf2 is by far the funniest line in the chapter... just that only we get a laugh! The White king is left exposed on f2. Now 11...Qh4+ is a very tricky check.} 11...Qh4+ 12.Ke3 {If the king tries to run, desperatly hoping for an escape via d3-c2... Qh6+ is a powerful check which forces the king back.} 12...Qh6+ 13.Kf2 {Kf2 is forced. Now Qf4+ comes with devastating effects. The king is forced onto the back rank!} 13...Qf4+ 14.Kg1 {The king is cornered. Now Ne2+ and the knight must be taken by the bishop! We'll win the queen next move!} 14...Ne2+ 15.Bxe2 {The bishop has to take. It's the only legal move.} 15...Qxa4 {White has lost the queen, and will probably have a tough time developing that queenside. By the time they manage to move, you should be able to mate the king or simply gain more material.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Doesn't Take The Bishop (6.Kf1) / Study 1: 6.Kf1 Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/7babac54-4126-47de-9a3f-ddd6aad8d117/studies/4eca023d-3b94-463d-b05b-6da99faf0c72/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study you'll learn how to crush people with the Traxler in the positions that may arise after we take on f2 and White goes 6.Kf1. White starts off in the most traditional way, pushing the king's pawn in the center. We'll mirror White's opening with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight, targeting the e5 pawn. We'll develop our knight as well, defending e5.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The Italian game, one of the most popular openings at any level. We develop our other knight to f6, attacking the e4-pawn.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {White decides to enter the Fried Liver! ...Bc5 is The Traxler counterattack!} 4...Bc5 5.Nxf7 {This is our second study looking at 5.Nxf7. It looks like we have too many pieces hanging, but we'll sacrifice another to put the White king in danger: Bxf2+!} 5...Bxf2+ 6.Kf1 {In this chapter we will analyze the positions arising from Kf1, which is the best move, but White's position remains in peril. It's not the most common move, however, played 41% of the time, which is 662 thousand games. White is using our bishop as a shield. Let's go ahead and remove our queen from danger.} 6...Qe7 7.Nxh8 {White decides to go up a rook by capturing on h8. Now that we we have sacrificed the rook, let's focus on attacking the king! 7...d5 opens the position even more. Our light-squared bishop is unleashed with a tempo!} 7...d5 8.exd5 {White recaptures and gains a tempo on our knight. We'll jump into the center with our knight, with the threat of Bg4, trapping White's queen.} 8...Nd4 9.d3 {d3 is the most played move. It opens the dark-squared bishop and creates an escape on d2 for the white queen. Bg4 is still a very strong developing move.} 9...Bg4 10.Qd2 {Qd2 is the only move which prevents the loss of the queen. Now our f2 bishop is really threatened! Now's the time to save that bishop. Bh4 opens up the f-file for future action.} 10...Bh4 11.c3 {White decides to attack our knight pushing the c-pawn. But now we can jump into e4 with the knight, attacking the queen! Of course White can take...} 11...Ne4 12.dxe4 {But now the door's wide open! Let's go in. Qf6+, and we force White into a corner.} 12...Qf6+ 13.Kg1 {Danger levels! Pause and try to find the move for black! With Ne2+, we block the queen's vision of the f2 square.} 13...Ne2+ 14.Bxe2 {White had to take with the queen to avoid immediate checkmate.} 14...Qf2# {Checkmate in 14 moves!} *

