1.e4 {In this study you'll learn how to crush people with the Traxler in the positions that may arise after we take on f2 and White goes 6.Kf1. White starts off in the most traditional way, pushing the king's pawn in the center. We'll mirror White's opening with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight, targeting the e5 pawn. We'll develop our knight as well, defending e5.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The Italian game, one of the most popular openings at any level. We develop our other knight to f6, attacking the e4-pawn.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {White decides to enter the Fried Liver! ...Bc5 is The Traxler counterattack!} 4...Bc5 5.Nxf7 {This is our second study looking at 5.Nxf7. It looks like we have too many pieces hanging, but we'll sacrifice another to put the White king in danger: Bxf2+!} 5...Bxf2+ 6.Kf1 {In this chapter we will analyze the positions arising from Kf1, which is the best move, but White's position remains in peril. It's not the most common move, however, played 41% of the time, which is 662 thousand games. White is using our bishop as a shield. Let's go ahead and remove our queen from danger.} 6...Qe7 7.Nxh8 {White decides to go up a rook by capturing on h8. Now that we we have sacrificed the rook, let's focus on attacking the king! 7...d5 opens the position even more. Our light-squared bishop is unleashed with a tempo!} 7...d5 8.exd5 {White recaptures and gains a tempo on our knight. We'll jump into the center with our knight, with the threat of Bg4, trapping White's queen.} 8...Nd4 9.Kxf2 {Maybe White chooses to take the bishop now. We can land a check. This time we'll put the knight on g4.} 9...Ng4+ 10.Ke1 {Choosing to retreat to e1. But Qf6, threatening mate, causes problems down the f-file.} 10...Qf6 11.Rf1 {White obviously tries to prevent this... We'll check on h4.} 11...Qh4+ 12.g3 {White blocks it, but...} 12...Qxh2 {We can capture the recently undefended h2-pawn and continue with a vicious attack against the king.} *

