1.e4 {In this study you'll learn how to crush people with the Traxler in the positions that may arise after we take on f2 and White goes 6.Kf1. White starts off in the most traditional way, pushing the king's pawn in the center. We'll mirror White's opening with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight, targeting the e5 pawn. We'll develop our knight as well, defending e5.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The Italian game, one of the most popular openings at any level. We develop our other knight to f6, attacking the e4-pawn.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {White decides to enter the Fried Liver! ...Bc5 is The Traxler counterattack!} 4...Bc5 5.Nxf7 {This is our second study looking at 5.Nxf7. It looks like we have too many pieces hanging, but we'll sacrifice another to put the White king in danger: Bxf2+!} 5...Bxf2+ 6.Kf1 {In this chapter we will analyze the positions arising from Kf1, which is the best move, but White's position remains in peril. It's not the most common move, however, played 41% of the time, which is 662 thousand games. White is using our bishop as a shield. Let's go ahead and remove our queen from danger.} 6...Qe7 7.Nxh8 {White decides to go up a rook by capturing on h8. Now that we we have sacrificed the rook, let's focus on attacking the king! 7...d5 opens the position even more. Our light-squared bishop is unleashed with a tempo!} 7...d5 8.exd5 {White recaptures and gains a tempo on our knight. We'll jump into the center with our knight, with the threat of Bg4, trapping White's queen.} 8...Nd4 9.d6 {This continuation is White's best line. It's a key defensive resource, giving White access to f7. We'll take the pawn with our queen.} 9...Qxd6 10.Nf7 {White brings their knight out with a tempo. We'll deploy the queen to c5, where it is lined up against f2. It also attacks the white bishop.} 10...Qc5 11.d3 {White defends it. We develop our last minor piece, attacking the white queen.} 11...Bg4 12.Qd2 {White moves it to safety, attacking our bishop.} 12...Bh4 {We retreat! White still has a lot of problems to solve. The immediate issues are ...Be2+ and ...b5. Keep in mind you don't have to memorize the entirety of this continuation, but merely understand the ideas within this system.} *

