[Event "Chapter 4: Traxler Early Sidelines / Study 1: White's Other 5th Move Options / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/9e9ac423-70c7-44d3-8334-f61fe5a1d225/studies/e1e43274-9992-42ba-976e-b099f64623db/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the fourth study of the Traxler. Previously, we saw Bxf7+ on move 5. But what if White goes for something else on move 5, like castling, d4 trying to open the position up, d3 being timid, or just development with Nc3? White opens with the king's pawn. We mirror White's choice.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White develops with an eye on f7. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {White defends e4 and converges on f7! But we'll ignore the incoming attack and target f2!} 4...Bc5 5.d4 {White chooses to break in the center! But we have a move of our own! This move is played 1% of the time, but is extremely confrontational, so we need to learn how to play against it! Let's push with d5, attacking the bishop!} 5...d5 6.Bxd5 {The bishop can also take. We'll give an intermezzo check.} 6...Bb4+ 7.c3 {White blocks it. We'll take the bishop on d5.} 7...Nxd5 8.exd5 {White recaptures. But this allows us to activate our queen.} 8...Qxd5 9.cxb4 {White takes our bishop.} 9...Qxg2 {But we take on g2, attacking the rook! White can easily go wrong here. Qf3 is the only real defense, and the queens will come off. But we'll still be causing trouble with moves such as ...e4 and Nxb4, giving us active compensation for our piece deficit.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Traxler Early Sidelines / Study 1: White's Other 5th Move Options / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/9e9ac423-70c7-44d3-8334-f61fe5a1d225/studies/e1e43274-9992-42ba-976e-b099f64623db/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the fourth study of the Traxler. Previously, we saw Bxf7+ on move 5. But what if White goes for something else on move 5, like castling, d4 trying to open the position up, d3 being timid, or just development with Nc3? White opens with the king's pawn. We mirror White's choice.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White develops with an eye on f7. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {White defends e4 and converges on f7! But we'll ignore the incoming attack and target f2!} 4...Bc5 5.d4 {White chooses to break in the center! But we have a move of our own! This move is played 1% of the time, but is extremely confrontational, so we need to learn how to play against it! Let's push with d5, attacking the bishop!} 5...d5 6.dxc5 {Should White take our bishop... We'll take theirs.} 6...dxc4 7.Qxd8+ {And after the trade...} 7...Nxd8 {We recapture with the knight and have an equal endgame.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Traxler Early Sidelines / Study 1: White's Other 5th Move Options / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/9e9ac423-70c7-44d3-8334-f61fe5a1d225/studies/e1e43274-9992-42ba-976e-b099f64623db/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the fourth study of the Traxler. Previously, we saw Bxf7+ on move 5. But what if White goes for something else on move 5, like castling, d4 trying to open the position up, d3 being timid, or just development with Nc3? White opens with the king's pawn. We mirror White's choice.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White develops with an eye on f7. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {White defends e4 and converges on f7! But we'll ignore the incoming attack and target f2!} 4...Bc5 5.O-O {White chooses safety before the attack! Around 6% of players don't want to get into the tricky Traxler lines. That's about 200,000 games. We'll also castle. We're now guarding f7!} 5...O-O 6.d3 {d3 supports e4 and prepares to develop the bishop on c1. We'll play d6 and do likewise.} 6...d6 7.Nc3 {White develops a knight. And we kick the other knight back.} 7...h6 8.Nf3 {After they retreat... We'll create a pin.} 8...Bg4 9.h3 {White tries to remove the pin. But we can just maintain it.} 9...Bh5 10.g4 {And g4 is a mistake. We can sacrifice a piece and maintain the pin.} 10...Nxg4 11.hxg4 {White takes the knight.} 11...Bxg4 {But the pin is revived and our knight can even jump to d4 next move to attack the pinned knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Traxler Early Sidelines / Study 1: White's Other 5th Move Options / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/9e9ac423-70c7-44d3-8334-f61fe5a1d225/studies/e1e43274-9992-42ba-976e-b099f64623db/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the fourth study of the Traxler. Previously, we saw Bxf7+ on move 5. But what if White goes for something else on move 5, like castling, d4 trying to open the position up, d3 being timid, or just development with Nc3? White opens with the king's pawn. We mirror White's choice.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White develops with an eye on f7. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {White defends e4 and converges on f7! But we'll ignore the incoming attack and target f2!} 4...Bc5 5.d3 {White attempts to be solid, supporting their center with d3. 5.d3 is only played 1% of the time. But we can get our king to safety and then question White's knight on g5.} 5...O-O 6.O-O {White also castles. We'll prepare to develop our bishop.} 6...d6 7.c3 {White plays c3, in preparation of d4. Let's kick the knight out.} 7...h6 8.Nf3 {The knight retreats... And we'll pin it to the queen.} 8...Bg4 9.h3 {White attacks us. We'll maintain the pin.} 9...Bh5 10.g4 {White cannot stubbornly remove the pin. We'll simply hit them with the knight sacrifice on g4.} 10...Nxg4 11.hxg4 {After recapturing...} 11...Bxg4 {We can maintain the pin to much success.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Traxler Early Sidelines / Study 1: White's Other 5th Move Options / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/9e9ac423-70c7-44d3-8334-f61fe5a1d225/studies/e1e43274-9992-42ba-976e-b099f64623db/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the fourth study of the Traxler. Previously, we saw Bxf7+ on move 5. But what if White goes for something else on move 5, like castling, d4 trying to open the position up, d3 being timid, or just development with Nc3? White opens with the king's pawn. We mirror White's choice.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White develops with an eye on f7. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {White defends e4 and converges on f7! But we'll ignore the incoming attack and target f2!} 4...Bc5 5.Nc3 {White goes for piece development, but it's questionable whether this is what the position requires. This move is only played 1% of the time and doesn't seem natural, because it gives us time to castle, which we'll do straightaway!} 5...O-O 6.d3 {White pushes d3. We'll push d6 ourselves.} 6...d6 7.Nd5 {White offers a minor piece trade. But we'll aim to trade off White's good bishop on c4.} 7...Na5 8.Nxf6+ {If White continues trading down...} 8...Qxf6 {We develop the queen while attacking f2. Next, we can push the knight back with ...h6, or seize the bishop pair by capturing on c4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Traxler Early Sidelines / Study 1: White's Other 5th Move Options / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/9e9ac423-70c7-44d3-8334-f61fe5a1d225/studies/e1e43274-9992-42ba-976e-b099f64623db/lines"]

1.e4 {This is the fourth study of the Traxler. Previously, we saw Bxf7+ on move 5. But what if White goes for something else on move 5, like castling, d4 trying to open the position up, d3 being timid, or just development with Nc3? White opens with the king's pawn. We mirror White's choice.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks e5. We'll defend the pawn.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White develops with an eye on f7. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.Ng5 {White defends e4 and converges on f7! But we'll ignore the incoming attack and target f2!} 4...Bc5 5.d4 {White chooses to break in the center! But we have a move of our own! This move is played 1% of the time, but is extremely confrontational, so we need to learn how to play against it! Let's push with d5, attacking the bishop!} 5...d5 6.exd5 {Should White take the pawn, attacking our knight... We'll get the knight out of danger and remove the attacker of our bishop, too.} 6...Nxd4 7.O-O {White gets to safety, but... We can develop with a tempo on the queen.} 7...Bg4 8.Qd2 {White attempts to move out of danger. But we'll kick the knight.} 8...h6 9.Nh3 {And once they retreat the knight... We can take it.} 9...Bxh3 10.gxh3 {If White recaptures...} 10...Nf3+ {That's a fork. We win White's queen!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 4.d3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/181297c1-d2fe-4a9b-aade-e5e481343c1d/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter we'll go over some sidelines in the Italian Game. In particular, what if we can't employ our Traxler because white doesn't go for the Fried Liver attack? In this study, we'll see what to do if White chooses to simply defend the e4 pawn with 4.d3. We begin with White's opening 1.e4. We begin with White's opening 1.e4, and we'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking e5. We'll develop, defending the e-pawn.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White brings the bishop out in standard Italian fashion. We'll bring our knight out, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d3 {In this study we'll break down what to do after the very solid d3, simply defending the pawn with another pawn. This move is played 28% of the time, which is more than 20 million games! In this position I suggest h6! This has two ideas: the defensive one is to take the g5 square away from a white piece. The offensive one is to prepare a g5 push!} 4...h6 5.O-O {White can choose to bring their king to safety. And they do so about a quarter of the time. We'll further support the e-pawn by playing ...d6.} 5...d6 6.c3 {White prepares d4. We'll expand with g5, now that White has castled!} 6...g5 7.Re1 {White supports e4 before pushing d4. This logical move is chosen 18% of the time. Let's continue marching on the kingside!} 7...g4 8.Nfd2 {White retreats. Our march goes on with h5!} 8...h5 9.d4 {White can strike in the center! In which case our assault may proceed with ...h4.} 9...h4 10.Nf1 {The knight goes back to defend. After all this time, we'll fianchetto!} 10...Bg7 11.Bg5 {White pins us! Let's attack the white bishop with ...Rh5!} 11...Rh5 12.Qc1 {The queen defends. We'll bring our queen forward in preparation for getting our king to safety.} 12...Qe7 13.Bd5 {White can attempt to trade a piece.} 13...Bd7 {But we'll just develop and be ready to castle queenside. Our kingside attack is already well underway.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 4.d3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/181297c1-d2fe-4a9b-aade-e5e481343c1d/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter we'll go over some sidelines in the Italian Game. In particular, what if we can't employ our Traxler because white doesn't go for the Fried Liver attack? In this study, we'll see what to do if White chooses to simply defend the e4 pawn with 4.d3. We begin with White's opening 1.e4. We begin with White's opening 1.e4, and we'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking e5. We'll develop, defending the e-pawn.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White brings the bishop out in standard Italian fashion. We'll bring our knight out, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d3 {In this study we'll break down what to do after the very solid d3, simply defending the pawn with another pawn. This move is played 28% of the time, which is more than 20 million games! In this position I suggest h6! This has two ideas: the defensive one is to take the g5 square away from a white piece. The offensive one is to prepare a g5 push!} 4...h6 5.c3 {Alternatively, c3 indicates that White would like to push d4. It's played 16% of the time. Thus here we'll support our central pawn and open up our light-squared bishop.} 5...d6 6.Nbd2 {White continues their development. Based on how the game is developing, considering White is quite solid, I suggest we play g6, not g5, to secure our bishop on g7. Additionally, g6 controls the f5 square.} 6...g6 7.d4 {White breaks in the center! Let's continue with our plan.} 7...Bg7 8.O-O {White castles... And so will we.} 8...O-O 9.dxe5 {White can choose to take in the center. But we can recapture with the pawn.} 9...dxe5 10.a4 {White proceeds with queenside expansion. We'll bring our queen out, supporting our pawn, while vacating the d8 square for a rook.} 10...Qe7 11.b4 {White continues expanding. We'll bring our rook to d8.} 11...Rd8 12.Qc2 {White steps out of danger. Let's mobilize our knight on the kingside. It's headed for f4 with attacking ideas.} 12...Nh5 13.Re1 {White begins establishing a defensive setup. We can transfer our queen to the kingside to apply pressure.} 13...Qf6 14.Nf1 {White brings a knight to f1 in order to have more defenders around their king. We'll attack f3.} 14...Bg4 15.N3d2 {The f3 knight retreats.} 15...Nf4 {Now from this position onwards, we have the idea of playing our bishop back to e6, trading it and recapturing with the queen, in order to infiltrate the kingside with our queen.} *

