1.e4 {In this chapter we'll go over some sidelines in the Italian Game. In particular, what if we can't employ our Traxler because white doesn't go for the Fried Liver attack? In this study, we'll see what to do if White chooses to simply defend the e4 pawn with 4.d3. We begin with White's opening 1.e4. We begin with White's opening 1.e4, and we'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking e5. We'll develop, defending the e-pawn.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White brings the bishop out in standard Italian fashion. We'll bring our knight out, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d3 {In this study we'll break down what to do after the very solid d3, simply defending the pawn with another pawn. This move is played 28% of the time, which is more than 20 million games! In this position I suggest h6! This has two ideas: the defensive one is to take the g5 square away from a white piece. The offensive one is to prepare a g5 push!} 4...h6 5.O-O {White can choose to bring their king to safety. And they do so about a quarter of the time. We'll further support the e-pawn by playing ...d6.} 5...d6 6.c3 {White prepares d4. We'll expand with g5, now that White has castled!} 6...g5 7.Nbd2 {White supports e4 with the knight. It's played 12% of the time at this point. Let's just fianchetto.} 7...Bg7 {Let's just fianchetto.} 8.Bb3 {White retreats to preserve their bishop against any attacks. Let's get our king to safety.} 8...O-O 9.Nc4 {White goes to the nice c4 square with the knight. This move makes a d5 push slightly more difficult for us. Let's develop, hitting that knight and opposing White's bishop.} 9...Be6 10.Re1 {White supports e4 with the rook. Likewise, we align our rook to the defense of e5.} 10...Re8 11.a4 {White expands on the queenside. But now we'll strike in the center with d5!} 11...d5 12.Ncxe5 {The knight gets out of danger and takes our pawn! We take the knight.} 12...Nxe5 13.Nxe5 {White recaptures, as they should. But now we can continue capturing, opening up the position favorably for our pieces.} 13...dxe4 14.d4 {White can advance and defend their knight. 14.dxe4 was impossible because we could take the bishop on b3 first, and then capture the knight on e5. We'll play to undermine the pawn.} 14...c5 15.a5 {White ignores c5 and continues pushing. We'll give White an isolated queen's pawn.} 15...cxd4 16.cxd4 {White recaptures, now with the IQP.} 16...Rc8 {Now we take control of the c-file. As is apparent in the current position, Black has good major piece activity. Meanwhile White struggles to find a plan, whereas we can continue with piling up the pressure on the isolated pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 4.d3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/181297c1-d2fe-4a9b-aade-e5e481343c1d/lines"]

1.e4 {In this chapter we'll go over some sidelines in the Italian Game. In particular, what if we can't employ our Traxler because white doesn't go for the Fried Liver attack? In this study, we'll see what to do if White chooses to simply defend the e4 pawn with 4.d3. We begin with White's opening 1.e4. We begin with White's opening 1.e4, and we'll reply with ...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking e5. We'll develop, defending the e-pawn.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {White brings the bishop out in standard Italian fashion. We'll bring our knight out, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d3 {In this study we'll break down what to do after the very solid d3, simply defending the pawn with another pawn. This move is played 28% of the time, which is more than 20 million games! In this position I suggest h6! This has two ideas: the defensive one is to take the g5 square away from a white piece. The offensive one is to prepare a g5 push!} 4...h6 5.O-O {White can choose to bring their king to safety. And they do so about a quarter of the time. We'll further support the e-pawn by playing ...d6.} 5...d6 6.c3 {White prepares d4. We'll expand with g5, now that White has castled!} 6...g5 7.Re1 {White supports e4 before pushing d4. This logical move is chosen 18% of the time. Let's continue marching on the kingside!} 7...g4 8.Nfd2 {White retreats. Our march goes on with h5!} 8...h5 9.Nf1 {White brings pieces for the defensive task. We'll enter the g-file, preventing the bishop from going to g5, and supporting our pawns going forward!} 9...Rg8 10.Be3 {White develops the bishop. We'll continue to expand and play ...h4.} 10...h4 11.g3 {White puts a stop to our advance. But we'll just develop normally.} 11...Bd7 12.b4 {White expands on the queenside. Meanwhile we'll develop the queen and get ready to castle.} 12...Qe7 13.Nbd2 {White concludes developing their queenside minor pieces.} 13...Nd8 {The knight is rerouted with the intent of going to the kingside! In the future, we might play ...Rh8 and ...Bh6 to trade for White's bishop on e3 and place the knight on g5.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 2: White Tries 4.d4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/99717bb4-97bb-4b5f-8f00-aa7c9cb89ceb/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the early sidelines. Presently, we'll look at the Max Lange Attack, which occurs after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.d4! White strikes early in the center with aggressive ideas. Let's see how to counter this early deviation! White begins with 1.e4, and we'll reply in kind.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking our pawn. We'll defend with the knight.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop comes out to play. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d4 {And White strikes immediately in the center! This move is played only 7% of the time under 2000 Elo, but that's still almost 5 million games. Here I suggest we take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.e5 {White advances, chasing our knight from f6! This is the most popular move, chosen 38% of the time in this position. How about we chase their bishop away from c4 in return?} 5...d5 6.Bb5 {White leaves c4 and pins our knight. And we leave f6 and settle our knight on e4.} 6...Ne4 7.Nxd4 {White mounts pressure on the pinned piece. But we develop without a care, playing the aggressive Bc5!} 7...Bc5 8.Be3 {Be3 supports the d4 knight and counters the c5 bishop on the diagonal. Here we can safely castle.} 8...O-O 9.Nxc6 {White goes for the pawn. We'll recapture.} 9...bxc6 10.Bxc5 {White trades off bishops before capturing the pawn. We'll accept the trade.} 10...Nxc5 11.Bxc6 {Now white takes on c6, forking our rook and pawn. But we'll step out of the way, attacking b2.} 11...Rb8 12.Qxd5 {After the queen takes our pawn...} 12...Qe7 {We come to e7, defending our knight. In the following moves we'll be able to bring our rooks to the open files and wreak havoc on White's position, despite being a pawn down.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 2: White Tries 4.d4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/99717bb4-97bb-4b5f-8f00-aa7c9cb89ceb/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the early sidelines. Presently, we'll look at the Max Lange Attack, which occurs after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.d4! White strikes early in the center with aggressive ideas. Let's see how to counter this early deviation! White begins with 1.e4, and we'll reply in kind.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking our pawn. We'll defend with the knight.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop comes out to play. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d4 {And White strikes immediately in the center! This move is played only 7% of the time under 2000 Elo, but that's still almost 5 million games. Here I suggest we take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.e5 {White advances, chasing our knight from f6! This is the most popular move, chosen 38% of the time in this position. How about we chase their bishop away from c4 in return?} 5...d5 6.Bb5 {White leaves c4 and pins our knight. And we leave f6 and settle our knight on e4.} 6...Ne4 7.Nxd4 {White mounts pressure on the pinned piece. But we develop without a care, playing the aggressive Bc5!} 7...Bc5 8.Nxc6 {In this variation White tries to win material. But we can take on f2 with check and make White lose castling rights!} 8...Bxf2+ 9.Kf1 {White moves out of check. Now we recapture on c6.} 9...bxc6 10.Bxc6+ {White checks us and attacks our rook. We slide over to f8. The trick is that White cannot take our rook because we've got 11...Ba6+ in reserve.} 10...Kf8 11.c4 {That's why White tries to be clever and blocks off the diagonal from our bishop. In which case we can be persistent in attacking! The queen comes to h4!} 11...Qh4 12.Bxa8 {Capturing our rook still isn't possible... Because we retreat the bishop, threatening checkmate on f2.} 12...Bc5 13.Qf3 {White attempts to defend, but...} 13...Bg4 {Bg4 removes the queen from the defence of f2, and things are getting nasty.} *

