1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the early sidelines. Presently, we'll look at the Max Lange Attack, which occurs after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.d4! White strikes early in the center with aggressive ideas. Let's see how to counter this early deviation! White begins with 1.e4, and we'll reply in kind.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking our pawn. We'll defend with the knight.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop comes out to play. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d4 {And White strikes immediately in the center! This move is played only 7% of the time under 2000 Elo, but that's still almost 5 million games. Here I suggest we take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.e5 {White advances, chasing our knight from f6! This is the most popular move, chosen 38% of the time in this position. How about we chase their bishop away from c4 in return?} 5...d5 6.Bb5 {White leaves c4 and pins our knight. And we leave f6 and settle our knight on e4.} 6...Ne4 7.Nxd4 {White mounts pressure on the pinned piece. But we develop without a care, playing the aggressive Bc5!} 7...Bc5 8.O-O {White can choose to castle as the knight is sufficiently defended. We'll castle as well!} 8...O-O 9.Nxc6 {White can go for the pawn here, with a tempo on our queen. We'll recapture with a tempo on their bishop.} 9...bxc6 10.Bxc6 {The bishop recaptures, attacking our rook. But we can counterattack their rook!} 10...Ba6 11.Bxa8 {White can go for the exchange of the rooks. So we acquiesce and take a rook ourselves.} 11...Bxf1 12.Kxf1 {Now the king recaptures, leaving White with pressure on d5 still.} 12...Qh4 {But we can just bring the queen out and attack f2 and h2. We are much better developed than White.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 2: White Tries 4.d4 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/99717bb4-97bb-4b5f-8f00-aa7c9cb89ceb/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the early sidelines. Presently, we'll look at the Max Lange Attack, which occurs after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.d4! White strikes early in the center with aggressive ideas. Let's see how to counter this early deviation! White begins with 1.e4, and we'll reply in kind.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking our pawn. We'll defend with the knight.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop comes out to play. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d4 {And White strikes immediately in the center! This move is played only 7% of the time under 2000 Elo, but that's still almost 5 million games. Here I suggest we take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.O-O {Castling! Offering the central pawns in exchange for activity of the pieces. Expect to face this move 28% of the time. Let's go ahead and take the e-pawn, and see how White wants to proceed.} 5...Nxe4 6.Re1 {Re1 is the most logical move, pinning our knight and getting ready to accumulate pressure on it. And you'll have to play against it by far the most often, 73% of the time. In reply, d5 attacks the bishop on c4 and defends our knight!} 6...d5 7.Bxd5 {White removes the defender of our knight. We'll recapture with the queen.} 7...Qxd5 8.Nc3 {White takes advantage of the pins! Our pawn is pinned and so is our knight on e4. But here we just fall back with the queen to d7.} 8...Qd7 9.Rxe4+ {Capturing with the rook gives a check right away. This is also met with Be7 blocking off the e-file.} 9...Be7 10.Nxd4 {White can capture, getting another piece into the center. And here we can strike at the central pieces with f5!} 10...f5 11.Rf4 {Rf4 gets out of danger but also removes the pin. Castling is now sensible. We also defend our f5 pawn with the rook.} 11...O-O 12.Nxc6 {White exchanges a pair of knights. For what purpose? The rook looks misplaced on f4. We take back with the queen.} 12...Qxc6 13.Nd5 {After Nd5 threatening our bishop but most importantly a check on e7 winning our queen...} 13...Bd6 {We bring the bishop up one square, where we still defend e7. Next, we'll develop normally with ...Be6, and try to get a rook to the d- or the e-file.} *

