1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the early sidelines. Presently, we'll look at the Max Lange Attack, which occurs after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.d4! White strikes early in the center with aggressive ideas. Let's see how to counter this early deviation! White begins with 1.e4, and we'll reply in kind.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking our pawn. We'll defend with the knight.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop comes out to play. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d4 {And White strikes immediately in the center! This move is played only 7% of the time under 2000 Elo, but that's still almost 5 million games. Here I suggest we take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.O-O {Castling! Offering the central pawns in exchange for activity of the pieces. Expect to face this move 28% of the time. Let's go ahead and take the e-pawn, and see how White wants to proceed.} 5...Nxe4 6.Re1 {Re1 is the most logical move, pinning our knight and getting ready to accumulate pressure on it. And you'll have to play against it by far the most often, 73% of the time. In reply, d5 attacks the bishop on c4 and defends our knight!} 6...d5 7.Bxd5 {White removes the defender of our knight. We'll recapture with the queen.} 7...Qxd5 8.Nc3 {White takes advantage of the pins! Our pawn is pinned and so is our knight on e4. But here we just fall back with the queen to d7.} 8...Qd7 9.Nxe4 {Capturing with the knight sets up discoveries! Here we block the open file with our bishop.} 9...Be7 10.Bg5 {White pressurizes our bishop on e7 but... We can just get our king out of the e-file by castling.} 10...O-O 11.Bxe7 {Once White chooses to trade... We'll just recapture with the queen.} 11...Qxe7 12.Nxd4 {White recovers another pawn.} 12...Bd7 {No issues, we just continue to develop. We have a bishop versus a knight in an open board.} *

