1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the early sidelines. Presently, we'll look at the Max Lange Attack, which occurs after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.d4! White strikes early in the center with aggressive ideas. Let's see how to counter this early deviation! White begins with 1.e4, and we'll reply in kind.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking our pawn. We'll defend with the knight.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop comes out to play. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d4 {And White strikes immediately in the center! This move is played only 7% of the time under 2000 Elo, but that's still almost 5 million games. Here I suggest we take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.e5 {White advances, chasing our knight from f6! This is the most popular move, chosen 38% of the time in this position. How about we chase their bishop away from c4 in return?} 5...d5 6.Bb5 {White leaves c4 and pins our knight. And we leave f6 and settle our knight on e4.} 6...Ne4 7.Nxd4 {White mounts pressure on the pinned piece. But we develop without a care, playing the aggressive Bc5!} 7...Bc5 8.O-O {White can choose to castle as the knight is sufficiently defended. We'll castle as well!} 8...O-O 9.Nxc6 {White can go for the pawn here, with a tempo on our queen. We'll recapture with a tempo on their bishop.} 9...bxc6 10.Bxc6 {The bishop recaptures, attacking our rook. But we can counterattack their rook!} 10...Ba6 11.Qxd5 {I suppose White could take the d-pawn as they've got sufficient pressure. But we can take that rook.} 11...Bxf1 12.Qxe4 {White could delay capturing the rook by taking our knight first.} 12...Bb5 {But here we've got the awesome retreating move Bb5, giving our bishop in view of a check on d1 with our queen, followed by capturing on c1.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 2: White Tries 4.d4 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/99717bb4-97bb-4b5f-8f00-aa7c9cb89ceb/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the early sidelines. Presently, we'll look at the Max Lange Attack, which occurs after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.d4! White strikes early in the center with aggressive ideas. Let's see how to counter this early deviation! White begins with 1.e4, and we'll reply in kind.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking our pawn. We'll defend with the knight.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop comes out to play. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d4 {And White strikes immediately in the center! This move is played only 7% of the time under 2000 Elo, but that's still almost 5 million games. Here I suggest we take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.O-O {Castling! Offering the central pawns in exchange for activity of the pieces. Expect to face this move 28% of the time. Let's go ahead and take the e-pawn, and see how White wants to proceed.} 5...Nxe4 6.Nc3 {This is the Nakhmanson Gambit! Try not to be greedy, and just simplify to your benefit. You'll encounter this gambit 13% of the time. We will play the safer Nxc3. Taking with the pawn allows crazy stuff from White, like the lines after 7.Bxf7.} 6...Nxc3 7.bxc3 {White recaptures as the queen was under attack. We'll develop and block the e-file.} 7...Be7 8.cxd4 {White regains a pawn. And now we'll play d5 ourselves to stop white from harassing us with d5.} 8...d5 9.Bb5 {White pins us for one move.} 9...O-O {And we get castled with an extra pawn!} *

