[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/99717bb4-97bb-4b5f-8f00-aa7c9cb89ceb/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the early sidelines. Presently, we'll look at the Max Lange Attack, which occurs after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.d4! White strikes early in the center with aggressive ideas. Let's see how to counter this early deviation! White begins with 1.e4, and we'll reply in kind.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking our pawn. We'll defend with the knight.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop comes out to play. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d4 {And White strikes immediately in the center! This move is played only 7% of the time under 2000 Elo, but that's still almost 5 million games. Here I suggest we take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.e5 {White advances, chasing our knight from f6! This is the most popular move, chosen 38% of the time in this position. How about we chase their bishop away from c4 in return?} 5...d5 6.exf6 {Should White take our piece...} 6...dxc4 {We can take their bishop. We have an extra pawn and the bishop pair!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 2: White Tries 4.d4 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/99717bb4-97bb-4b5f-8f00-aa7c9cb89ceb/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the early sidelines. Presently, we'll look at the Max Lange Attack, which occurs after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.d4! White strikes early in the center with aggressive ideas. Let's see how to counter this early deviation! White begins with 1.e4, and we'll reply in kind.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking our pawn. We'll defend with the knight.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop comes out to play. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d4 {And White strikes immediately in the center! This move is played only 7% of the time under 2000 Elo, but that's still almost 5 million games. Here I suggest we take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.O-O {Castling! Offering the central pawns in exchange for activity of the pieces. Expect to face this move 28% of the time. Let's go ahead and take the e-pawn, and see how White wants to proceed.} 5...Nxe4 6.Re1 {Re1 is the most logical move, pinning our knight and getting ready to accumulate pressure on it. And you'll have to play against it by far the most often, 73% of the time. In reply, d5 attacks the bishop on c4 and defends our knight!} 6...d5 7.Nc3 {What about this insane move, leaving two pieces hanging? Let's just ignore the material and get more piece development in. Be6 blocks the rook's view of our king.} 7...Be6 8.Bxd5 {White can choose to remove a defender of our knight. We'll take on c3.} 8...dxc3 9.Bxe4 {After White gets the piece back... We'll simply trade queens.} 9...Qxd1 10.Rxd1 {White recaptures. We'll trade off pawns on b2.} 10...cxb2 11.Bxb2 {White develops with a view to g7. So we'll block the diagonal!} 11...f6 12.Re1 {White places their rook opposite our king, so... We'll remove our rook from the potential pin and defend our bishop with the king.} 12...Kf7 13.Bc3 {White controls more squares with the bishop on c3.} 13...Bc5 {And we ourselves develop our bishop, meeting any rooks to b1 with ...Bb6. Then we'll look to activate the rooks. We are a safe pawn up.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 2: White Tries 4.d4 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/99717bb4-97bb-4b5f-8f00-aa7c9cb89ceb/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of the early sidelines. Presently, we'll look at the Max Lange Attack, which occurs after 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Nf6 4.d4! White strikes early in the center with aggressive ideas. Let's see how to counter this early deviation! White begins with 1.e4, and we'll reply in kind.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops, attacking our pawn. We'll defend with the knight.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop comes out to play. We'll develop, attacking e4.} 3...Nf6 4.d4 {And White strikes immediately in the center! This move is played only 7% of the time under 2000 Elo, but that's still almost 5 million games. Here I suggest we take on d4.} 4...exd4 5.O-O {Castling! Offering the central pawns in exchange for activity of the pieces. Expect to face this move 28% of the time. Let's go ahead and take the e-pawn, and see how White wants to proceed.} 5...Nxe4 6.Re1 {Re1 is the most logical move, pinning our knight and getting ready to accumulate pressure on it. And you'll have to play against it by far the most often, 73% of the time. In reply, d5 attacks the bishop on c4 and defends our knight!} 6...d5 7.Nc3 {What about this insane move, leaving two pieces hanging? Let's just ignore the material and get more piece development in. Be6 blocks the rook's view of our king.} 7...Be6 8.Nxe4 {White captures on e4 straight up. Let's take the pesky bishop instead of the knight.} 8...dxc4 9.Neg5 {White pins our bishop because of the discovered attack from their rook, and mounts pressure on e6.} 9...Be7 {Let's just develop to get out of danger.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 3: Italian Game 4th Move Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/d53cee3c-f142-407a-88d3-438d9ae51e5b/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we'll go over some deviations which aren't particularly good for White, but you might indeed see them in an actual game, especially at lower levels. We're talking about move 4 choices by White which we can exploit. These moves are 4.0-0/c3/Nc3. So let's begin with 1.e4 e5!} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks e5. We'll develop and defend it.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop is taken out in standard Italian fashion. And we'll develop and attack e4.} 3...Nf6 4.c3 {What about c3, preparing d4? It's only played 4% of the time, and that's because... We can simply take the unprotected e-pawn.} 4...Nxe4 5.d4 {White can continue with their original idea of d4! Let's play the same old move we're used to, attacking the bishop and defending our knight.} 5...d5 6.Bb5 {White pins us. Let's take once on d4 and preserve our extra pawn.} 6...exd4 7.Qxd4 {White can take with the queen as our knight is pinned. We'll unpin it, while making sure d5 is still protected.} 7...Qd7 {We unpin it.} 8.Bxc6 {White takes the attacker. We'll recapture with the queen.} 8...Qxc6 9.O-O {White castles. But before we can develop our f8 bishop to castle ourselves, we need to cover g7. Hence f6.} 9...f6 10.Qd3 {White gets away from the d4 square, where our bishop to c5 could have attacked the queen. We'll develop it anyway.} 10...Be7 11.Nd4 {Our queen comes under attack. But we can retreat to d7.} 11...Qd7 12.Re1 {Re1 places the rook opposite the king for one move.} 12...O-O {We secure our king. In the future, ...c5 and ...c4 might be in the cards. We'll look to improve the position of the dark-squared bishop, probably to c5, and develop the rest of the pieces. We've also maintained the extra pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 3: Italian Game 4th Move Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/d53cee3c-f142-407a-88d3-438d9ae51e5b/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we'll go over some deviations which aren't particularly good for White, but you might indeed see them in an actual game, especially at lower levels. We're talking about move 4 choices by White which we can exploit. These moves are 4.0-0/c3/Nc3. So let's begin with 1.e4 e5!} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks e5. We'll develop and defend it.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop is taken out in standard Italian fashion. And we'll develop and attack e4.} 3...Nf6 4.c3 {What about c3, preparing d4? It's only played 4% of the time, and that's because... We can simply take the unprotected e-pawn.} 4...Nxe4 5.O-O {White can pretend they'll be dynamic and get activity for the pawn. We've got to be used to this move by now, attacking the bishop and defending our knight on e4.} 5...d5 6.Bb5 {White moves over, pinning our knight. We'll develop and defend e5.} 6...Bd6 7.Re1 {White brings the rook opposite our king. We'll just get out of danger and castle.} 7...O-O 8.d3 {After d3, removing our knight from e4... We can retreat to the active c5 square.} 8...Nc5 9.Bxc6 {White removes a defender of e5. We'll recapture.} 9...bxc6 10.Nxe5 {White takes on e5. We'll bring our queen to an attacking position in front of the white king.} 10...Qh4 11.Nf3 {Retreating attacks our queen and defends h2.} 11...Qh5 {But we maintain the queen on the h-file to always keep an eye on h2, while threatening Bg4.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 3: Italian Game 4th Move Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/d53cee3c-f142-407a-88d3-438d9ae51e5b/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we'll go over some deviations which aren't particularly good for White, but you might indeed see them in an actual game, especially at lower levels. We're talking about move 4 choices by White which we can exploit. These moves are 4.0-0/c3/Nc3. So let's begin with 1.e4 e5!} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks e5. We'll develop and defend it.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop is taken out in standard Italian fashion. And we'll develop and attack e4.} 3...Nf6 4.O-O {What if White just castles, contending that perhaps Re1 after we capture on e4 will give them sufficient counterplay? This move is played 10% of the time, which is around 7.5 million games. We are happy to test our their idea! We'll capture the pawn.} 4...Nxe4 5.Bd5 {Now this is the top computer move, forking both knights, one of which (the knight on c6) is the defender of e5. Even though it's only played 4% of the time, you might just face it against a prepared opponent. We can retreat, attacking the bishop.} 5...Nf6 6.Bxc6 {The bishop takes the defender of e5. We'll recapture away from the center to open up our queen and light-squared bishop.} 6...dxc6 7.Nxe5 {White regains the pawn. But we will develop with a tempo on the knight.} 7...Bd6 8.d4 {White defends it. We'll get safely castled.} 8...O-O 9.Bg5 {Play could continue with white pinning us. Here we can ask the bishop a question.} 9...h6 10.Bh4 {But they can choose to maintain the pin. We'll add another attacker to the knight on e5.} 10...Re8 11.Nc3 {White develops the knight with the idea of jumping to e4 and applying pressure on our pinned knight.} 11...Bf5 {We therefore develop the bishop and cover e4. We have a comfortable position.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 3: Italian Game 4th Move Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/d53cee3c-f142-407a-88d3-438d9ae51e5b/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we'll go over some deviations which aren't particularly good for White, but you might indeed see them in an actual game, especially at lower levels. We're talking about move 4 choices by White which we can exploit. These moves are 4.0-0/c3/Nc3. So let's begin with 1.e4 e5!} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks e5. We'll develop and defend it.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop is taken out in standard Italian fashion. And we'll develop and attack e4.} 3...Nf6 4.O-O {What if White just castles, contending that perhaps Re1 after we capture on e4 will give them sufficient counterplay? This move is played 10% of the time, which is around 7.5 million games. We are happy to test our their idea! We'll capture the pawn.} 4...Nxe4 5.Re1 {White can bring the rook to the e-file, opposite our king. Expect to see this move about half the time. The response to this is to defend the knight and attack the bishop at once!} 5...d5 6.Bb5 {Once White pins us... We can develop, converging on f2!} 6...Bc5 7.d4 {White blocks the diagonal and our attack on f2. I don't know if you're counting, but this is a second pawn for us.} 7...exd4 8.Nbd2 {White pressurizes our knight on e4. Here I suggest castling and getting to safety, as we'll already up two pawns. We can give one up for the sake of safety and development.} 8...O-O 9.Nxe4 {White initiates the sequence that wins a pawn back. We'll recapture.} 9...dxe4 10.Rxe4 {And White gets one pawn back.} 10...Qd5 {We can develop the queen with a tempo. We're a pawn up with a better position.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Italian Game Sidelines / Study 3: Italian Game 4th Move Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/72ddb8b5-0907-484e-82a9-b82fb1d28b6b/chapters/ba15f5ce-e489-4584-94da-89c33eec77d7/studies/d53cee3c-f142-407a-88d3-438d9ae51e5b/lines"]

1.e4 {In this study we'll go over some deviations which aren't particularly good for White, but you might indeed see them in an actual game, especially at lower levels. We're talking about move 4 choices by White which we can exploit. These moves are 4.0-0/c3/Nc3. So let's begin with 1.e4 e5!} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White attacks e5. We'll develop and defend it.} 2...Nc6 3.Bc4 {The bishop is taken out in standard Italian fashion. And we'll develop and attack e4.} 3...Nf6 4.Nc3 {What about this logical move, defending e4? It's played 16% of the time. We can take it anyways!} 4...Nxe4 5.Nxe4 {Once White captures, we've got the famous "center fork trick."} 5...d5 {d5 ensures we'll regain the piece, while being a useful move to develop our queen and light-squared bishop.} *